WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.542
Boolean masks are one of the most useful ideas in NumPy,

00:00:03.542 --> 00:00:06.517
and you will see the same pattern again in Pandas.

00:00:06.517 --> 00:00:09.052
We begin with an array of scores.

00:00:09.052 --> 00:00:12.312
Then we compare the entire array to ninety.

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:19.322
The expression scores greater than or equal to ninety does not return one True or False value.

00:00:19.322 --> 00:00:24.032
It returns an array of Boolean values, one for each score.

00:00:24.032 --> 00:00:26.687
Click Run and look at the mask.

00:00:26.687 --> 00:00:32.517
Positions where the condition is true are marked True, and the other positions are False.

00:00:32.517 --> 00:00:36.567
Then we place that mask inside square brackets.

00:00:36.567 --> 00:00:40.367
NumPy keeps only the values where the mask is True.

00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:44.077
The result is a new array containing the high scores.

00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:48.142
We can also use size to count how many values were selected.

00:00:48.142 --> 00:00:51.527
This pattern is extremely common in analytics.

00:00:51.527 --> 00:00:57.272
We might select transactions above a threshold, measurements outside a normal range,

00:00:57.272 --> 00:01:01.577
customers with high activity, or model scores above a cutoff.

00:01:01.577 --> 00:01:04.851
Think of a Boolean mask as a reusable filter.
